package com.pathland.view;

import com.pathland.view.signal.WritableSignal;

/**
 * An audio style ({@code AudioStyle}). A style turns an {@link Audio}
 * node's media configuration (its playback signals) into a body view. Styles are
 * injected down the tree via {@code View.audioStyle(AudioStyle)}.
 *
 * <p>The default {@link NativeAudioStyle} contributes no content (the
 * {@link EmptyContent} sentinel) — the renderer shows native controls. A custom
 * style returns the app-defined control UI (transport buttons, seek/volume
 * sliders) bound to the configuration's signals; the {@link Audio} control
 * attaches it as the {@code AUDIO} node's child, so the renderer emits a hidden
 * media element + the custom controls (spec DSL.md §5.7).
 */
public interface AudioStyle extends Style {

    /** Build the styled content for {@code config}. */
    View makeBody(Configuration config);

    /**
     * The media configuration of the audio being styled: the playback signals a
     * custom control UI binds to. The source rides the {@code Audio} view itself.
     *
     * @param playing  the play/pause state (writable — a control toggles it)
     * @param position the play position in seconds (writable — a seek slider)
     * @param volume   the volume 0..1 (writable — a volume slider)
     * @param duration the media length in seconds (the seek slider's maximum)
     */
    record Configuration(WritableSignal<Boolean> playing, WritableSignal<Float> position,
                         WritableSignal<Float> volume, float duration) {}
}

parts.text:
The spec §5.7/§5.5 says a style IS a ViewModifier value that binds the env key. Currently there's a separate `*StyleMod` wrapper. To make `.modifiers(ButtonStyle)` work, `Style` should extend `ViewModifier` and each style's `body()` should bind the env key. That's the spec's target.

However, this is a bigger design change (styles currently are pure interfaces with `makeBody`, and the `*Mod` classes do the env binding). Implementing `body()` on the style interface requires knowing which env key — each style type knows its key. So `ButtonStyle.body(content)` → `content.environment(Environment.BUTTON_STYLE, this)`.

Java interfaces can have default methods. So:
```java
public interface ButtonStyle extends Style {
    View makeBody(Configuration config);
    @Override default View body(View content) {
        return content.environment(Environment.BUTTON_STYLE, this);
    }
    record Configuration(View label) {}
}
```
And `Style extends ViewModifier`.

Then `.modifiers(ButtonStyle)` works, and `*StyleMod` classes become redundant (can be removed later). Keep them for bridge.

But `View.environment(key, value)` exists (View sugar). Good.

Let me do this for ButtonStyle, LabelStyle, AudioStyle, VideoStyle: add `default View body(View content)`.

And `Style extends ViewModifier`.

This is high-value (the spec's style-as-modifier). Let me do it.

But wait: `Environment.BUTTON_STYLE` etc. are static keys. `content.environment(Environment.BUTTON_STYLE, this)` — `View.environment(EnvironmentKey<T>, T)`. `this` is ButtonStyle. Fine.

Let me edit Style to extend ViewModifier, and add `body` defaults to the 4 styles.

Also `ColorPicker`/etc fine.

Let me edit.

Style:
```java
public interface Style extends ViewModifier {
}
```

ButtonStyle: add import? Same package. Add:
```java
@Override
default View body(View content) {
    return content.environment(Environment.BUTTON_STYLE, this);
}
```

LabelStyle: `Environment.LABEL_STYLE`.
AudioStyle: `Environment.AUDIO_STYLE`.
VideoStyle: `Environment.VIDEO_STYLE`.

Let me edit these.

But `Style extends ViewModifier` means `ViewModifier.body(View)` is abstract; each concrete style must implement it (default provided). Good.

Let me do the edits.

